Z||000000||glava2876||05-20-2001||12:12 PM||||I removed the tank from my Fiero because after a very long storage period, I added drygas, fuel injection cleaner and fresh fuel,it ran and then quit.Figured out that the fuel pump sock was plugged -pump ran, bur fuel just barely dribbled. I cleaned the tank, replaced the fuel pump, pump filter, and fuel pump. Filled it with gas, turned it over and still no fuel. Starts with gas in the TBI, so I have ignition. I left it in disgust and haven't run through any diagnostics yet. I figure I'll pull the fuel line, run it into a jug and jumper the fuel pump to see if it is pushing fuel. If yes, can the pressure regulator stick closed and cut off all fuel? If pump (New AC Delco)isn't pushing fuel, there must be a problem in the lines? The pump had a grey and a black wire and required cutting the old wires and splicing with crimp connectors. I understood the intructions to indicate that the black goes to the line and the gray to the one hat connects as a ground? If wired wrong, could the pump run backwards? Was there anything else that should have been done prior to trying to start the engine? I hate fuel injection problems!! Thanks to anyone that can head me in the right direction. Take care, Glen||||reg||5
Z||000001||Rodrv6||05-20-2001||02:24 PM||||Good idea to check for flow like you described.  A fuel pressure regulator stuck closed will cause excessively high pressure, so that's probably not your problem.  When you changed the pump, did you change the short piece of rubber tubing that connects the pump with the fuel line?  I had the one one my 84 split and that caused fuel starvation since the pump was just pushing the gas right out into the tank.  It acted just like a bad pump.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Wolfhound||05-20-2001||02:48 PM||||Glen, the pump I recently replaced was Gray Postive and Black Ground. It wasn't AC Delco but black is usually ground.<BR> I don't want to cause you unnessary work with bad advice. The fuel line check you mentioned seems like a good idea and should tell you if it,s running backward.<BR>Coy||||reg||1
Z||000003||glava2876||05-20-2001||03:10 PM||||yes, I did change the rubber piece -as a matter of fact, the old one was soft and looked to have a hole. Anyone else have an idea if the wiring can be screwed up and have the pump run backwards. I don't have the paper work here, it's with the car at another location, but I can swear it indicated to go black to the feed -the originals are both black!. Thanks, I hope to tinker again Monday night. Glen||||reg||1
Z||000004||glava2876||05-21-2001||06:48 PM||||Pulled the line at the filter today & jumpered the pump and no fuel. Disconnected the line at the sender & jumpered the pump and no fuel. Blew through the upper section and it is clear. I know I have to pull the tank again, but what will the problem be? It has to be a plugged line in the sending unit or a problem with the new AC fuel pump? No one has really responded to my question regarding the fuel pump wiring -if I mixed the gray and black wire could the pump possibly work backwards? Thanks, Glen||||reg||5
